  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level low currently spinning over the Great Lakes will\nshift east tonight and move overhead tomorrow bringing with it \nrain showers and cooler conditions. Most guidance shows a dry \nslot being drawn northward and wrapping up into this system, \nkeeping showers more isolated in coverage for the Twin Tiers on \nsouth. Additional scattered showers are expected to continue \ninto Saturday night, especially during the evening, before the \nupper low shifts east and eventually northeast along the New \nEngland Coast. There looks to be a short lull in shower activity\nSunday, especially during the midday and afternoon as weak \nridging briefly builds in before the next shortwave trough \narrives Sunday night into Monday. \n\nA noticeably cooler, more fall-like day will be in store \ntomorrow with highs from the mid 40s to the mid 50s, then with \nthe weak ridging and more westerly flow on Sunday, high \ntemperatures do recover a bit with highs in the 50s to near 60 \ndegrees. Overnight lows will be in the 30s and 40s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs mentioned above, the next shortwave trough will be moving \ninto the region Sunday night into Monday with another round of \nshowers. There could be some wet snowflakes that mix in prior to\ndaybreak Monday in the highest elevations of the Catskills and \nnorthern Oneida County. There is also a bit of uncertainty in \nterms of how widespread the shower coverage may be on Monday as \nthe Euro and Canadian deterministic and ensemble guidance are \nstronger and farther south with this system, while the GFS is \nfarther north and less potent, so that will be something to \nmonitor.\n\nAnother break in the wet weather looks to be from late Monday \nthrough Tuesday night or Wednesday. Then a cool and potentially \nunsettled pattern takes hold of the region the rest of the week \nas an upper trough settles in over the eastern U.S. with \nscattered rain showers during the day and rain/snow showers at \nnight. \n\nHighs are expected to be in the 50s and 60s Monday through \nWednesday before settling back in the 40s late week. Overnight \nlows look to drop back into the 20s mid-to-late week.",
